{"id":"aero_colors","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::aero_colors","source_path":"aero_colors/aero_colors.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"This graph shows basic handling of an aero color property for colored smoke effects.","documentation_file":"aero_colors_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Aero Colors\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses additional custom properties with aero sources to create colored smoke.\n\n### Inputs and outputs\n\nThe input for this graph is a noisy volume density compound that is used as source geometry for an aero simulation. The output is the resulting aero simulation volume with color data.\n\n### Important note\nThe colorful aero smoke can be seen only when using the Arnold Viewport or Arnold output render, and requires a light other than the `defaultLightSet`. However one can visualize the non-shaded colors by enabling the diagnostic draw on the `voxel_color_scope`.\n\n### How it works\n\n- The `noise_density` compound converts a torus to a volume and combines that with a fractal noise field volume to create a noise torus of density, which is used for the aero source. One could substitute any desired geometry for source_air.\n\n- An `aero_source_color_property` is used to create an additional property `voxel_color`, which is initialized using a `fractal_turbulence_field` inside `color_field` to create a range of colors.\n\n- The output of the aero solve is connected to an `aero_color_post_process` node that keeps colors from going dark during simulation.\n\n- To render out the color data contained in the aero output via the `voxel_color` custom color property, an aiStandardVolume shader is assigned and set to use the `default_bifrost_aero` preset along with specifying the property name `voxel_color` for the Scatter Color Channel.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[],"terminal_states"